timestamp=2026-11-04T16:01:20.867Z level=INFO run=b196681e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# So the onset order by physical time is: TURBINE (16:58) -> STEAM_GEN (21:59) -> COOLING (00:09 next day, but wait 00:09 is 2024-01-16 00:09:00) -> PRIMARY_LOOP (04:32) -> CORE (09:36)\n# Looking at the dates: 16:58 on 2024-01-15, then 21:59 on 2024-01-15 (so ~5 hours later), then 00:09 on 2024-01-16 (a few hours after that), etc.\n# That gives a cascade order: TURBINE, STEAM_GEN, COOLING, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE.\n# But let us be more careful - does this make physical sense? The reactor has interconnected subsystems: COOLING, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE, STEAM_GEN, TURBINE. \n# Also \"failure that originated in one subsystem propagated through physical connections to the others, eventually affecting all five\"\n# Usually in PWR: core heats primary loop; primary loop transfers heat via steam generator to secondary (steam) side; steam drives turbine. Cooling system (often reactor coolant system / auxiliary cooling/residual heat removal or condenser cooling etc.) is involved in heat removal. Also feedwater, etc. \n# If turbine fails first (loss of load, trip, or mechanical issue), that can cause pressure/temperature transients - e.g. turbine trip leads to steam pressure rise, affecting steam generator and primary loop. That can trigger scrams etc. Or if it’s a loss of condenser cooling, different effects. But we need to look at the data to infer.\n# Also alarms started at 18:00:00Z with TURB_OUTPUT_01 WARNING, dropping to 770 MW. That matches turbine output dropping early. Then operator actions: at 2024-01-16 02:52:00Z EMERGENCY_SHUTDOWN,TURBINE. So turbine was shut down/emergency shutdown around 02:52. Then other actions later.\n# Our physical onset shows TURBINE first (16:58), then STEAM_GEN (21:59), then COOLING (00:09 16th), etc. That sequence could be plausible depending on coupling.\n# Let us look deeper into the transitions - are there other early anomalies? Also look at the exact values more. For example, STEAM_PRES_01 first phys deviation at 21:59 - that’s a few hours after turbine output dropped at 16:58. That’s consistent with turbine trip causing steam pressure changes.\n# Also COOLING deviations start around 00:09 (COOLING_PRES_01). PRIMARY_LOOP starts deviating later at 04:32. CORE at 09:36. That means the cascade propagated through steam generator to cooling system, then to primary loop, then to core? Or some other path. Let us verify the early signals more carefully.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
