timestamp=2026-11-04T16:03:02.142Z level=INFO run=b196681e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Based on earliest physical deviation per subsystem: TURBINE (16:58) is first, then STEAM_GEN (21:59), then COOLING (00:09 16th), then PRIMARY_LOOP (04:32), then CORE (09:36). That gives order [TURBINE, STEAM_GEN, COOLING, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE].\n# Let us look at operator actions and alarms in context. Operator actions: EMERGENCY_SHUTDOWN TURBINE at 02:52:00Z (which is 02:52 UTC). The sensor timestamps in dataset are local times? We need to be consistent. Let us check the time offsets in metadata: logger_utc_offset_hours varies - TURBINE has offset -1, CORE has +2, COOLING has 0, PRIMARY 0, STEAM_GEN +2. So timestamps in the CSVs are in logger local time according to that? Or are they all recorded in a common timebase?\n# \"logger_utc_offset_hours\" is given per sensor. The timestamps in sensor files - need to interpret. Also alarms.csv and operator_actions.csv might be in UTC (Z suffix). Let us look at actual relationships.\n# For example, TURB_OUTPUT_01 has offset -1. Observed deviation at 17:00:00 (local logger time for that sensor?). And physical onset is 16:58 local? That’s just math. But when correlating with operator actions at 02:52:00Z UTC, we need to convert to a common frame. Let us convert everything to UTC consistently.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
