timestamp=2026-11-04T16:03:32.659Z level=INFO run=b196681e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Better to treat sensor timestamps as given, but apply offsets to get UTC if needed?\n# \"logger_utc_offset_hours\" - offset of logger relative to UTC. So if logger time is L and offset is +2 (logger is 2 hours ahead of UTC), then UTC = L - offset*hours, or L = UTC + offset. So logger_utc_offset_hours: e.g. +2 means logger is UTC+2, so local logger time = UTC + 2h, hence UTC = logger_time - 2h.\n# Check with examples. CORE has offset +2, TURBINE has offset -1. Alarms and ops are in UTC (Z suffix, we converted). \n# Alarms first at 2024-01-15 18:00:00 UTC (after conversion we got 2024-01-15 18:00:00 as naive UTC). \n# TURB_OUTPUT physical onset was 16:58:00 in logger local time? Because timestamps in sensor files are the logger’s timestamps. And TURB_OUTPUT’s logger offset is -1, meaning logger is UTC-1 (1 hour behind UTC), so logger_time = UTC - 1h, so UTC = logger_time + 1h. That means physical onset at 16:58:00 (logger time) is 17:58:00 UTC.\n# Also alarms for TURB_OUTPUT started at 18:00:00 UTC. That’s about 2 minutes after physical onset at 17:58 UTC (16:58 + 1h). That fits: physical state began deviating at 17:58 UTC, observed deviation appeared at 18:00:00 UTC (delay 2 min), and alarm triggered around then. That matches perfectly. So to compare with ops/alarms in UTC, we must convert sensor times to UTC.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
