timestamp=2026-11-04T16:04:26.970Z level=INFO run=b196681e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# With UTC conversion:\n# TURBINE: first phys UTC = 2024-01-15 17:58:00\n# STEAM_GEN: 2024-01-15 19:59:00 (was 21:59 local with offset +2; 21:59 - 2 = 19:59 - correct)\n# COOLING: 00:09:00 UTC (offset 0) - same\n# PRIMARY_LOOP: 04:32:00 UTC (offset 0) - same\n# CORE: 07:36:00 UTC (was 09:36 local with offset +2; 09:36 - 2 = 07:36)\n\n# Now compare to ops: \n# 2024-01-16 02:52:00Z - EMERGENCY_SHUTDOWN, TURBINE\n# 2024-01-16 03:35:00Z - INCREASE_COOLING, COOLING\n# 2024-01-16 05:08:00Z - ADJUST_FEEDWATER, STEAM_GEN\n# 2024-01-16 08:12:00Z - REDUCE_POWER, PRIMARY_LOOP\n# 2024-01-16 15:56:00Z - SCRAM, CORE\n\n# Alarms started at 18:00:00Z with TURB_OUTPUT warnings - which is after turbine physical onset at 17:58:00Z. That fits.\n\n# The cascade: TURBINE first (17:58Z), then STEAM_GEN (19:59Z), then COOLING (00:09Z next day), then PRIMARY_LOOP (04:32Z), then CORE (07:36Z). \n# The physical connections: steam is generated in steam generator and drives turbine. If something happens at turbine (e.g. turbine trip/load rejection), that immediately affects steam system (backpressure rises, steam pressure changes). That explains TURBINE -> STEAM_GEN. \n# Cooling system in PWR - condenser cooling, or possibly component cooling / reactor coolant system related. But cooling subsystem is listed separately. Also operators increased cooling at 03:35Z, and cooling’s physical onset is at 00:09Z (before that action). \n# How does turbine issue propagate to cooling? Could be via condenser (turbine exhaust to condenser) - loss of cooling to condenser can cause issues, or if turbine trips, maybe cooling load changes. Or perhaps the event affects multiple systems? Or is the origin in cooling? That seems less likely given turbine output dropped first.\n# Or another interpretation: loss of cooling (e.g. loss of heat sink) can cause primary loop temperatures to rise, leading to steam generator pressure changes and turbine issues. Let us look at the actual trends.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
