timestamp=2026-11-04T16:06:26.336Z level=INFO run=b196681e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# PRIMARY_TEMP_01’s observed values are fluctuating; normal range is 280-320. Values go up to ~320 and maybe beyond? Let us see the exact deviation threshold crossing. Also look at the full deviation detection again - we marked deviating when value < normal_min OR value > normal_max. 320.55 is > 320.0, so it should be deviating. But is it just barely over? And does it stay? Also first_obs_deviation was 2024-01-16 04:37:00 local, which is 04:37Z - that gives phys onset 04:32Z (delay 5 min). That’s correct.\n# Also compare to actual values: look for when it definitively crosses threshold consistently. But the question says \"when its physical state first began to deviate from normal\". The earliest persistent deviation is what matters - and our approach gives us that. The sequence we got based on first physical onset per subsystem is plausible.\n# But let us consider alternative hypotheses. Another common scenario: loss of cooling (cooling system fails) leads to rise in primary loop temperature, then steam generator issues, then turbine issues. That would be COOLING -> PRIMARY_LOOP -> STEAM_GEN -> TURBINE. That’s the opposite direction from what we see.\n# But in our data, turbine output drops first (17:58Z). That points to turbine as the initiator, causing steam pressure changes (19:59Z). That’s consistent with turbine trip. Then downstream effects propagate - possibly condenser/cooling affected? And reactor/primary loop responds later, core later. That fits.\n# Also operator actions: emergency shutdown of turbine at 02:52Z (after turbine already degraded). Then increase cooling at 03:35Z. Then adjust feedwater at 05:08Z (affecting steam gen). Then reduce power in primary loop at 08:12Z. Then scram at 15:56Z. The order of operator actions is TURBINE, COOLING, STEAM_GEN, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE? Wait look at the exact times and subsystems: \n# 02:52Z - TURBINE (emergency shutdown). 03:35Z - COOLING (increase cooling). 05:08Z - STEAM_GEN (adjust feedwater). 08:12Z - PRIMARY_LOOP (reduce power). 15:56Z - CORE (scram). So operator actions order by time is TURBINE, COOLING, STEAM_GEN, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE. That’s interesting - operators acted on TURBINE first, then COOLING, then STEAM_GEN, then PRIMARY_LOOP, then CORE. That ordering of subsystems by operator response time roughly matches the propagation order of physical degradation? Because they respond to symptoms as they become apparent/serious. That’s a strong hint. Also compare our physical onset order: TURBINE (17:58Z), STEAM_GEN (19:59Z), COOLING (00:09Z), PRIMARY_LOOP (04:32Z), CORE (07:36Z). So by onset time: TURBINE, STEAM_GEN, COOLING, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE. That’s very close to operator action order: TURBINE, COOLING, STEAM_GEN, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE. But STEAM_GEN and COOLING are swapped in position between onset times? Let us look: onset times - STEAM_GEN is at 19:59Z (day 15 evening), COOLING is at 00:09Z (day 16 early morning). So STEAM_GEN physical onset happens before COOLING. Operators acted on COOLING at 03:35Z before STEAM_GEN at 05:08Z? 03:35Z is before 05:08Z, so operator action order is TURBINE (02:52), COOLING (03:35), STEAM_GEN (05:08). That means operators responded to cooling issues before steam generator issues. But the physical state of STEAM_GEN began deviating earlier than COOLING (19:59Z vs 00:09Z). That seems contradictory? Or maybe the deviation in steam gen was not yet severe enough to trigger obvious alarms/actions, or the symptoms operators prioritized were in cooling first? Let us look at alarms and severity.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
