timestamp=2026-11-04T16:06:51.424Z level=INFO run=b196681e message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\n\n# Alarm first times (UTC, naive): TURBINE 18:00, STEAM_GEN 20:00, COOLING 00:10 (16th), PRIMARY_LOOP 05:56 (16th), CORE 11:26 (16th). That gives order TURBINE, STEAM_GEN, COOLING, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE. Same as physical onset order we derived after UTC correction.\n# Operator actions by time: TURBINE 02:52, COOLING 03:35, STEAM_GEN 05:08, PRIMARY_LOOP 08:12, CORE 15:56. So TURBINE first, then COOLING, then STEAM_GEN - so COOLING and STEAM_GEN swapped relative to alarm/physical onset. But the question asks for the full causal propagation order based on physical state deviation, not operator response. The key phrase: \"determine the full causal propagation order — rank all five subsystems from the one whose physical state first began deviating from normal operating conditions to the one that was affected last. The onset time of a subsystem is when its physical state first began to deviate from normal, not when sensors detected the deviation...\"\n# So the correct order is the order of first physical onset times. From our analysis with proper UTC handling and accounting for response delays: TURBINE (17:58:00Z) -> STEAM_GEN (19:59:00Z) -> COOLING (00:09:00Z) -> PRIMARY_LOOP (04:32:00Z) -> CORE (07:36:00Z). That gives [TURBINE, STEAM_GEN, COOLING, PRIMARY_LOOP, CORE].\n# Let us also look at other sensors in TURBINE more. TURBINE’s other sensors have later physical onsets (bearings, speed, vibration start deviating physically later, around 01:00Z+). That suggests the initial physical deviation in the turbine subsystem manifested as output change first, then mechanical effects followed - which is consistent with a load/turbine event causing secondary effects. The subsystem-level onset is the earliest among its sensors, which is correct.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
